Patch 61 -- the island's translated gl_info must be used at EVERY GL call site.

WHAT WAS BROKEN

Patch 53 translates a guest `wined3d_gl_info` into a native copy once per device
and returns it from `MGS2_GL_INFO()`. `GL_EXTCALL(f)` was routed through that
macro, and patch 55 had converted a handful of individual sites by hand -- but
only those. Every GL call written as a plain member access --
`gl_info->gl_ops.gl.p_glFoo(...)`, `gl_info->fbo_ops.glBindFramebuffer(...)`,
`wined3d_context_gl_get_current()->gl_info->gl_ops...` -- read the GUEST table,
so in the ARM island it called an x86 address. There were 315 such sites.

`mgs2_island_gl_info()` also translated only `gl_ops`. `translated = *guest`
copies the whole structure, so `fbo_ops`, `ffp_attrib_ops`, `p_glDisableWINE`,
`p_glEnableWINE` and `p_wglCreateContextAttribsARB` kept guest pointers too.

HOW IT PRESENTED, AND WHY IT LOOKED LIKE SOMETHING ELSE

Arming island entry 34 (`wined3d_texture_load_location`) on 2026-08-19 killed
the renderer on the first frame with

    wine: Unhandled illegal instruction at address 4002013B

which is entry 34's own Box86 bridge + 0xB. That address is the bridge's `ret`,
and it is intact -- box86 prints `x86opcode=C3`. The dynarec parks the emulated
EIP there (`MOV32(xEIP, addr)` in dynarec_arm_00.c case 0xCC) BEFORE calling the
wrapper, so any host fault inside native ARM code is reported to Wine at that
address. With BOX86_SHOWSEGV=1 the real fault reads

    SIGILL @0x7aeccdf0 ... opcode=55 64 8B 0D 18 00 00 00

`55 64 8B 0D 18 00 00 00` is `push ebp; mov ecx, fs:0x18` -- an i386 prologue --
and 0x7aeccdf0 is inside the mounted `opengl32.dll`, in the unnamed thunk region
(nearest export `glFrustum` +0xac0). Native ARM had branched into x86 bytes.

Two hypotheses were checked and refuted first, so they need not be checked again:

  markers   entry 34's marker sits at +15 of its function and Box86 matched the
            function start exactly (`entry 34 matched 0x7b92c6c0`, which is the
            .eh_frame FDE start). Its duplicate at +68 is outside the 64-byte
            window and never matches
  layouts   every structure on the path is byte-identical between the shipping
            i386 build and the island's armhf build -- wined3d_texture 292,
            wined3d_texture_gl 468, wined3d_context_gl 1684, wined3d_resource
            168 -- with a control that trips as documented: texture_stage_op is
            16 against 12 and ffp_frag_settings 132 against 100

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES

  utils.c        translate fbo_ops by name (position is the name, as for
                 gl_ops), ffp_attrib_ops through the class A/B/C ladder, the two
                 glXxxWINE pointers likewise, and CLEAR
                 p_wglCreateContextAttribsARB so an island call through it fails
                 loudly -- context creation stays on the x86 side by design
  5 files        135 call sites rewritten to go through MGS2_GL_INFO():
                 utils.c 62, context_gl.c 26, texture_gl.c 25, gl_compat.c 21,
                 view.c 1

`adapter_gl.c` is deliberately NOT rewritten. Its 180 sites are adapter and
extension-table initialisation, which runs on the x86 side; several of them
ASSIGN to gl_ops/fbo_ops members through token-pasting macros, and routing those
through a const-returning accessor would both fail to compile and, if it did
compile, write native pointers into the guest's own table.

VERIFICATION

The i386 build must be unaffected, because `MGS2_GL_INFO(gi)` is `(gi)` there.
Checked rather than assumed: all 32 wined3d translation units compiled for i386
before and after, from the same source path so `__FILE__` cannot differ.
`.text`, `.rdata` and `.data` are byte-identical in every one; four objects
differ only in DWARF, because the rewritten expressions moved source columns.

RESULT ON THE DEVICE

The illegal instruction is gone. Entry 34 now fails the way the design intends,
legibly: `SIGSEGV @(nil) ... for accessing (nil)`, a call through an unresolved
GL slot left NULL. That is a different, smaller problem -- see
docs/briefs/MGS2_ISLAND_ENTRY34_FAULT_2026-08-19.md -- and it is what the
fail-closed preflight exists to catch before arming.

NOT MEASURED. This patch is on the diagnostic path only. No production binary
carries it, and no frame-rate claim is attached to it.

